{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 351b03ec and 700be209: accepted, with one change to custody. R3b source still waits for R3a's review request.\n\nThe bound: `reusable` holds at most 4,096 records and at most 8 MiB of accounted payload. The payload of a record is the measured UTF-8 bytes of its subject name, its observation, its read and last metadata, and every value copy that it stores. Measure the actual record. The 50,347-byte allowance is not a ceiling before insertion, as you found. On insertion or replacement, drop the oldest records until both ceilings hold. A replacement keeps its place in the order. A record above the byte ceiling serves its own input and is not kept. That insertion also removes an older record of the same name, so no later input reads the older answer. The docs say \"retained payload bytes\", not heap size.\n\nCustody, changed from your proposal: pin at first use. When `hand()` first gives an input a reusable record, the input keeps that reference to its end. Do not copy the map when `further()` starts. A copy costs up to 4,096 references for each input and shows the input the answers of its start instead of the answers of its first use, which is what main shows. The deliberate reread after a freshness or head failure stays as it is. If the source makes first-use pinning larger than a start snapshot, report the two diffs in lines before you choose.\n\nSource trust: accepted as a stated boundary. After a drop, a fresh read is monotonic because the production path reads the same Native object's append-only history. State that in the note and in the threat-model sentence. Add no high-water map.\n\nMake the bound easy to test. Give the bounded store its own small owner whose two ceilings are constructor arguments. Tests, three in all:\n1. A Node unit owner for the store with small ceilings: the count drop, the byte drop, a replacement that keeps its place, and an oversized record that is not kept and removes its older namesake.\n2. The signed Native witness as you describe it in 700be209: seed @una, 4,097 absent members in batches, real count and bytes read after each batch, then @una again inside 300 seconds with exactly one fresh read and the original accepted outcome.\n3. One control: unbounded insertion restored, and the witness fails on the real map size.\nAdd no other eviction test.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}